INT. FERRY TICKET BOOTH, DESERTED PIER - DUSK

A glass box no bigger than a phone booth, its paint flaking in sheets. Fog presses at the windows. ODALYS PRIETO, seventies, wrapped in a green cardigan, sits on a stool amid cardboard boxes marked in marker: LAST DAY.

On the counter, a battered cassette recorder with a masking-tape label. She hesitates, then presses PLAY.

A hiss. A younger woman's voice, warm and slightly breathless, fills the booth.

                    ODALYS (V.O.)
          Test, test. Okay. This is Odalys, and it's the first of June, and the little ones are asleep, so I will whisper the schedule.

Odalys closes her eyes. Her hand drifts across the counter and rests on a brass stamp.

                    ODALYS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
          Seven a.m., the fishermen. Eight, the schoolchildren, mind the puddles. Noon, nobody. Nobody ever, and that's when I read.

The tape hisses. Outside, a gull drops onto the pier rail and watches her.

                    ODALYS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
          If anyone ever hears this, I want you to know it was a good job. The best view in the county, and nobody to talk to but the tide.

Odalys smiles, then stops smiling. She slides the stamp into her cardigan pocket, switches off the lamp, and presses STOP. The silence rings.

                    ODALYS
          (to the empty booth)
          It was.

She pulls the shutter down.
